# Store and restore bytes exactly.
#
# core.autocrlf is true globally on this machine, so a checked-out file can
# come back with different bytes than went in. Next door in claude-config
# that silently rewrote 32 of 33 restored files, and the one file anybody
# thought to spot-check was the only one incapable of revealing it.
#
# memory.sql is the entire backup. A fact value containing a newline is
# perfectly legal and several will exist eventually, and an embedded newline
# rewritten on checkout is silent corruption of the store's contents rather
# than a cosmetic diff.
#
# Tested here before adding this: a multi-line value survived a real
# clone-and-restore. It survived for reasons the test could not fully
# explain -- statement separators came back CRLF while the newline inside
# the quoted string stayed LF. Depending on a mechanism nobody can account
# for is how the sibling repo got bitten, so this removes the question
# instead of trusting the observation.
#
# Verify with:  python test_roundtrip.py
* -text
